Not sourced from any document, observed in this session with stock
git 2.56.0 and the local build: a rebase that pauses at a break or an
edit stop and is finished with `git rebase --continue` copies notes
only when notes.rewriteRef is set for the process that runs the
continue. A `-c notes.rewriteRef` on the first rebase command does not
carry over, and every note on a rewritten commit is lost. A rebase
that runs straight through with the `-c` keeps them. That is why the
check prints the continue with the `-c` on it, and why it keeps
rebase-merge/rewritten-list: `git notes copy --stdin` fed that file
put the notes back in the test. With notes.rewriteRef in the global
config, as this host has it, the continue copies them by itself.

Signatures are detected by the gpgsig header rather than %G?, because
an SSH signature without an allowed-signers file does not verify.
gpg-signed commits were not tested.

Tested in scratch repos: prep on a published commit (exit 3, then
--allow-published), on a range with a fixup! (exit 4), on a dirty
tree, on a detached HEAD, on two commits on different lines of a
merge, on a root commit; check at a break, after the continue, at an
edit stop (exit 2), after a drop that removed a note, and after a
rebase without --update-refs that left a branch behind.
